{title:Title}

{pstd}{bf:crangejoin} {hline 2} Join observations whose keys fall within
specified intervals

{marker syntax}{...}

{title:Syntax}

{p 8 12 2}{cmd:crangejoin} {it:keyvar low high} {cmd:using} {it:filename}
[{cmd:,} {opt by(varlist)} {opt k:eepusing(varlist)} {opt p:refix(string)}
{opt s:uffix(string)} {opt a:ll} {opt threads(#)} {opt verbose}
{opt snap:shot} {opt fast}]

{marker description}{...}

{title:Description}

{* ctools-credit:begin (generated by scripts/sync_attribution.py; edit config/attribution.json)}{...}
{pstd}
{cmd:crangejoin} reimplements {cmd:rangejoin} 1.1.3 (April 13, 2021) by Robert
Picard. See {help crangejoin##acknowledgments:Acknowledgments} for details.
{* ctools-credit:end}{...}

{pstd}{cmd:crangejoin} joins observations by numeric intervals.
Each master row is paired with every using row whose numeric key is within its
inclusive bounds and whose by() values agree. Unmatched master rows remain with
missing using values. Master order is preserved; matches within a master row are
ordered by using key, with ties preserving their original using order.
The joined dataset replaces the data in memory. There is no if/in syntax;
filter the master dataset beforehand. The command does not store results in {cmd:r()} or {cmd:e()}.

{pstd}A bound can be a numeric master variable (absolute bounds) or a number.
Numeric bounds are offsets from the master key when it exists, and absolute
bounds otherwise. A missing lower/upper bound is unbounded in that direction.
Using rows with missing keys never match. If the master contains keyvar, its
missing keys never match, even with explicit bound variables. Reversed intervals
produce no match. As in rangejoin, no valid master intervals or no nonmissing
using keys returns error 2000.

{marker options}{...}

{title:Options}

{pstd}{opt by(varlist)} requires matched observations to have the same values
of the listed variables. Numeric and string variables are allowed. Missing
values can match, and each extended numeric missing value defines a separate
group. keyvar cannot also be a by() variable.

{pstd}{opt keepusing()} selects using variables. Key and by variables are always
loaded.

{pstd}{opt prefix()} and {opt suffix()} rename using variables sharing names
with master variables. The default is suffix(_U) when neither is specified.
{opt all} renames every using
variable except the by() variables. Name conflicts return an error.

{pstd}{opt threads(#)} sets the maximum number of threads, from 1 to 256.
Omitting it or specifying zero uses the {help ctools##threads:ctools setting}.
{opt verbose} reports the time spent loading data, sorting keys, identifying
groups and key values, finding and counting matches, and writing the joined
data.

{pstd}By default, an error or interruption restores the master dataset.
Recovery uses an in-memory copy on Stata 16 or later and {help preserve} on
earlier versions.

{pstd}{opt snapshot} uses {help preserve} for recovery on every Stata version.
Stata may write the copy to disk when it exceeds {cmd:c(max_preservemem)}.

{pstd}{opt fast} skips recovery. An error or interruption can leave a partially
joined dataset. It cannot be combined with {opt snapshot}.


{marker examples}{...}

{title:Examples}

{phang2}{cmd:. crangejoin date -5 5 using events.dta, by(firm)}{p_end}

{phang2}{cmd:. crangejoin price lower upper using houses.dta, keepusing(address)}{p_end}

{phang2}{cmd:. crangejoin date . . using history.dta, by(person) suffix(_history)}{p_end}

{title:Remarks}

{pstd}Every matching pair produces an output observation, so the joined dataset
can be much larger than either input. Inputs and output are limited to
2,147,483,647 observations. Numeric and fixed-width string variables are
supported, and so are {cmd:strL} master and using variables: the plugin
carries their observation numbers through the join and the values are copied
in afterwards. As in {cmd:rangejoin}, whose {cmd:merge} refuses them, {opt by()}
variables cannot be {cmd:strL} (error 106, with {cmd:merge}'s message).
{cmd:rangejoin} and {cmd:rangestat} are not required.


{* ctools-attribution:begin (generated by scripts/sync_attribution.py; edit config/attribution.json)}{...}
{marker acknowledgments}{...}
{title:Acknowledgments}

{pstd}
{cmd:crangejoin} reimplements {cmd:rangejoin}, written by Robert Picard. Its
syntax, interval specification, and output follow {cmd:rangejoin}.
{cmd:rangejoin} itself requires {cmd:rangestat}, written by Robert Picard,
Nicholas J. Cox, and Roberto Ferrer.

{pstd}
{cmd:crangejoin} is pinned to this version of {cmd:rangejoin}:

{p2colset 9 27 29 2}{...}
{p2col:Command}{cmd:rangejoin}{p_end}
{p2col:Authors}Robert Picard{p_end}
{p2col:Version}1.1.3{p_end}
{p2col:Date}April 13, 2021{p_end}
{p2col:Header line}{cmd:*! version 1.1.3, 13apr2021, Robert Picard}{p_end}
{p2col:Source}SSC ({cmd:ssc install rangejoin}), distributed April 15,
2021.{p_end}
{p2col:Latest version?}Yes, as of November 25, 2026.{p_end}
{p2colreset}{...}

{pstd}
{cmd:crangejoin} changes only when ctools is updated to a newer version of
{cmd:rangejoin} and tested against it. {cmd:which rangejoin} shows the version
installed on your machine. {help ctools##acknowledgments:help ctools} lists
the pinned versions of all ctools commands.

{pstd}
Please report bugs and questions about {cmd:crangejoin} at
{browse "https://github.com/mdroste/stata-ctools/issues":github.com/mdroste/stata-ctools/issues}.
The authors of {cmd:rangejoin} do not maintain {cmd:crangejoin}. Undocumented
differences between {cmd:crangejoin} and {cmd:rangejoin} are bugs in
{cmd:crangejoin}.

{pstd}
If you use {cmd:crangejoin} in published work, please cite the original
command:

{phang2}Picard, R. 2016. RANGEJOIN: Stata module to form pairwise combinations
if a key variable is within range. Statistical Software Components S458162,
Boston College Department of Economics. Revised April 15, 2021.{p_end}
